        <title type="html"><![CDATA[Chrome 145 letter-spacing and word-spacing percentage support]]></title>
        <id>https://github.com/mdn/browser-compat-data/pull/29200</id>
        <link href="https://github.com/mdn/browser-compat-data/pull/29200"/>
        <updated>2026-03-16T09:10:11.000Z</updated>
        <content type="html"><![CDATA[<img src="https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/47647?u=2fbfc431a85d357f00c0146d5e55b425b7f00a3d&v=4" width="64" height="64" alt=""/><br/>
<h4 dir="auto">Summary</h4>
<p dir="auto">Chrome 145 adds support for percentage values on the <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/Reference/Properties/letter-spacing" rel="nofollow"><code class="notranslate">letter-spacing</code></a> and <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/Reference/Properties/word-spacing" rel="nofollow"><code class="notranslate">word-spacing</code></a> properties.</p>
<p dir="auto">See <a href="https://chromestatus.com/feature/5106867491700736" rel="nofollow">https://chromestatus.com/feature/5106867491700736</a>.</p>
<p dir="auto">This PR adds Chrome support data for % values on both these properties. Note that <code class="notranslate">word-spacing</code> already had this data, but it was marked as deprecated. I'm wondering if it was deprecated a while ago. It certainly doesn't seem to be now, as specified in the <a href="https://www.w3.org/TR/css-text-4" rel="nofollow">CSS Text L4 spec</a>.</p>
<p dir="auto">I've marked both Firefox and Safari as supported, as in tests, the % values seem to work in both.</p>

